.TH "EVALRESP" "1" "2026-11-07" "evalresp 5.0.1" "EarthScope programs"
.SH "NAME"
\fIevalresp\fR \- evaluate the frequency response of instrument channels described in FDSN StationXML or SEED RESP
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.B evalresp
.I STA_LIST CHA_LIST YYYY DAY
[\fIMIN_FREQ\fR [\fIMAX_FREQ\fR [\fINFREQS\fR]]]
[\fB\-f\fR \fIfile\fR]
[\fB\-u\fR \fIunits\fR]
[\fB\-t\fR \fItime\-of\-day\fR]
[\fB\-s\fR \fItype\-of\-spacing\fR]
[\fB\-r\fR \fIresp\-type\fR]
[\fB\-n\fR \fInetwork\-id\fR]
[\fB\-l\fR \fIlocation\-id\fR]
[\fB\-stage\fR \fIstart\fR [\fIstop\fR]]
[\fB\-stdio\fR]
[\fB\-use\-estimated\-delay\fR]
[\fB\-unwrap\fR]
[\fB\-ts\fR]
[\fB\-il\fR | \fB\-ii\fR]
[\fB\-b62_x\fR \fIvalue\fR]
[\fB\-x\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
\fIevalresp\fR calculates the complex frequency response of one or more channels
at a given date and time, for a set of frequencies, and writes the result to
ASCII files.  The channels are described either by FDSN StationXML, as served
by the FDSN station web service, or by SEED RESP files, a legacy format
as written by \fIrdseed\fR.
.LP
Run without arguments, \fIevalresp\fR prints a short usage message.
.SH "ARGUMENTS"
.TP
.I STA_LIST
Station codes or patterns.  Separate several with commas or spaces.
.TP
.I CHA_LIST
Channel codes or patterns.  Separate several with commas or spaces.
.TP
.I YYYY
Year of the evaluation.
.TP
.I DAY
Day of the year, 1 to 366.
.TP
.I MIN_FREQ
Lowest frequency in Hz.  The default is 1.0.
.TP
.I MAX_FREQ
Highest frequency in Hz.  The default is \fIMIN_FREQ\fR.  If it is less than
\fIMIN_FREQ\fR the two are swapped.
.TP
.I NFREQS
Number of frequencies from \fIMIN_FREQ\fR to \fIMAX_FREQ\fR, inclusive.  The
default is 1.
.PP
The channels evaluated are those that match the stations, channels, network and
location and whose epoch includes the date and time given.  Options may follow
the arguments in any order.
.SH "OPTIONS"
Most options also have a long form, given in parentheses.  Options are written
with a single or a double leading hyphen.  Options that take a value read the
next word.
.TP
.BR \-f " " \fIfile\fR " (" \-file )
Input file, or a directory of RESP files.  See \fBINPUT\fR.
.TP
.BR \-u " " \fIunits\fR " (" \-units )
Units of the response: \fBdis\fR (displacement), \fBvel\fR (velocity),
\fBacc\fR (acceleration) or \fBdef\fR.  The default is \fBvel\fR.  With
\fBdef\fR the response is output units over input units, exactly as the first
stage of the response takes them in and the last stage gives them out.  This is
the choice for sensors whose units cannot be converted to displacement,
velocity or acceleration.  Pressure (Pa), temperature (degrees C) and magnetic
flux density (T) responses are always evaluated in their own units and this
option has no effect on them.
.TP
.BR \-t " " \fIHH:MM:SS\fR " (" \-time )
Time of day, used to choose between responses that are effective on the same
day.  The default is 00:00:00.
.TP
.BR \-s " " \fBlog\fR | \fBlin\fR " (" \-spacing )
Spacing of the frequencies between \fIMIN_FREQ\fR and \fIMAX_FREQ\fR.  The
default is \fBlog\fR, which cannot start at a frequency of zero.
.TP
.BR \-r " " \fBap\fR | \fBfap\fR | \fBcs\fR " (" \-response )
Type of output; see \fBOUTPUT\fR.  The default is \fBap\fR, or \fBfap\fR when
\fB\-stdio\fR is given.
.TP
.BR \-n " " \fInetwork\-id\fR " (" \-network )
Network code or pattern.  The default is \fB*\fR.
.TP
.BR \-l " " \fIlocation\-id\fR " (" \-location )
Location codes or patterns, separated by commas.  The default is \fB*\fR.
A blank location, such as \fB\-l "  "\fR, matches only channels with an empty
location code.
.TP
.BR \-stage " " \fIstart\fR " [" \fIstop\fR "]"
Evaluate only stage \fIstart\fR, or the stages \fIstart\fR through \fIstop\fR,
and ignore the others.  A \fIstart\fR below zero selects all stages, the
default.  If the stages requested are not in the response, nothing is written
and an error message is printed.
.TP
.BR \-stdio
Write to standard output instead of files and, without \fB\-f\fR, read the
input from standard input.
.TP
.BR \-use\-estimated\-delay " (" \-use\-delay )
Use the estimated delay (field 7 of blockette 57) instead of the correction
applied (field 8) when computing the phase of an asymmetric FIR filter.  Use
this only if the correction applied is known to be wrong in the response.
.TP
.BR \-unwrap
Unwrap the phase in \fBap\fR output.  Phase in \fBfap\fR output is always unwrapped.
.TP
.BR \-ts
Use the total sensitivity (stage 0) instead of the product of the stage gains.
Combined with \fB\-stage\fR this gives, for example, a sensor stage with the
correct system gain, as SAC poles and zeros do.
.TP
.BR \-il ", " \-ii
Interpolate a List blockette (55) to the requested frequencies; see
\fBLIST BLOCKETTE\fR.  The two options are the same.
.TP
.BR \-b62_x " " \fIvalue\fR
Value at which a polynomial blockette (62) is linearised; see \fBPOLYNOMIAL BLOCKETTE\fR.
.TP
.BR \-x " (" \-xml )
Expect StationXML.  By default the format is detected.
.TP
.BR \-v " (" \-verbose )
Print a summary of each response evaluated; see \fBMESSAGES\fR.
.SH "INPUT"
Two formats are read: FDSN StationXML (preferred) and SEED RESP (legacy).  A
file is detected as StationXML if it is XML, and as RESP otherwise; \fB\-x\fR
makes StationXML the only choice, and a file that is not XML then fails to parse.
StationXML values are used at the precision of the file.
.LP
Where the input is read from depends on \fB\-f\fR.
.TP
\fB\-f\fR \fIfile\fR, a file
Only that file is read, in either format.  It may hold any number of channels,
and for RESP may be a concatenation of RESP files.
.TP
\fB\-f\fR \fIdirectory\fR
Only that directory is searched, for RESP files named \fBRESP.NET.STA.LOC.CHA\fR
or \fBRESP.NET.STA.CHA\fR, where the parts match the network, station, location
and channel requested.  StationXML files in the directory are not read.
.TP
no \fB\-f\fR
The current directory and then the directory named by the \fBSEEDRESP\fR
environment variable are searched for RESP files as above.  When a channel is
found in both, the file in the current directory is used.
.PP
Wildcards allow several files to match one request, so every matching file is
searched.  A request that matches no channel writes nothing and still exits
with status 0; an unreadable file or an unmatched directory search prints a
message to standard error.
.LP
With \fB\-stdio\fR and no \fB\-f\fR, the input is read from standard input, in
either format, and every matching channel is evaluated until the end of input.
.LP
To make RESP files from a SEED volume, run \fIrdseed\fR (version 4.16 or later)
with its \fB\-R\fR option, or with \fB\-d\fR and answering yes when asked
whether to write response files.
\fIxml2resp\fR(1) converts StationXML to RESP.
.SH "CHANNEL SELECTION"
Stations, channels, networks and locations are matched as whole codes with
glob patterns, not regular expressions:
\fB*\fR matches any number of characters, \fB?\fR one character,
\fB[abc]\fR and \fB[a\-z]\fR one character from a set or range, \fB[!abc]\fR
one character not in it, and a backslash quotes the next character.
A pattern is at most 64 characters long.  Quote patterns from the shell, for
example \fB'BH?'\fR, so that the shell does not expand them.
.LP
Every combination of the stations, channels and locations listed is
requested, and each channel that matches one of them and is effective at the
date and time given is evaluated.  If more than one entry of a channel is
effective then, the shortest is used and a warning is printed.
.LP
A network of \fB''\fR, \fB'??'\fR or \fB'*'\fR matches a channel that has no
network code.
.SH "OUTPUT"
Each channel evaluated is written to its own files, named from its network,
station, location and channel codes.  The location part is empty if the channel
has none, as in \fBAMP.Z.CGV..HYZ\fR.  Frequencies are in Hz and numbers are in
the form 1.000000E\-03.  Existing files are overwritten.
.TP
\fB\-r ap\fR
\fBAMP.NET.STA.LOC.CHA\fR holds the amplitude and \fBPHASE.NET.STA.LOC.CHA\fR the
phase in degrees, each as two columns: frequency and value.
.TP
\fB\-r fap\fR
\fBFAP.NET.STA.LOC.CHA\fR holds three columns: frequency, amplitude and
unwrapped phase.
.TP
\fB\-r cs\fR
\fBSPECTRA.NET.STA.LOC.CHA\fR holds three columns: frequency, and the real and
imaginary parts of the response.
.PP
With \fB\-stdio\fR the same text is written to standard output, each channel
preceded by a banner line naming it.
.PP
The response is that of the whole channel, from the input of the first stage
to the output of the last, evaluated in the units chosen with \fB\-u\fR.
.SH "MESSAGES"
Errors, warnings and, with \fB\-v\fR, summaries of each response are written to
standard error, each with a date and a level, so they never mix with output on
standard output.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 if the evaluation succeeded, including when no channel matched; otherwise
non\-zero, with a message on standard error.
The values are 1 for a memory error, 2 for an input or output error such as an
unreadable file, 3 for invalid input such as a bad argument, 4 for an internal error,
5 for a file that cannot be parsed, and 7 for a response that fails validation.
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.TP
.B SEEDRESP
A directory searched for RESP files when \fB\-f\fR is not given.
.SH "RESPONSE EVALUATION"
.SS "Filter sequence"
A response is a cascade of stages.  The first filter of a stage is one of:
a Laplace or analog pole\-zero filter, an IIR pole\-zero filter, a FIR filter
(symmetric or asymmetric), an IIR coefficients filter, a List (generic
response) blockette, or a stand\-alone gain, which gives the sensitivity of the
whole sequence (stage 0).  A stage is normally followed by a gain, and has a
decimation blockette if it decimates.  The order of the blockettes within a
stage does not matter; they are put in the order evalresp expects.
.LP
For a single stage response the gain may be given as stage 0 alone, since it is
the same as the stage 1 gain.  A channel made only of gain stages evaluates to its gain.
.LP
The stage numbers must be in sequence, and a break in the sequence makes the
response invalid.  The output units of a stage must match the input units of
the next, or the response is rejected.  Stages that are only a gain have no
units and are skipped in this comparison: the units of the stage before one
are compared with those of the stage after it.
.SS "SEED conventions"
Since July 2007 SEED requires FIR coefficients to be listed in forward order,
so that a minimum\-phase filter, which is asymmetric, has its largest values at
the start.  It also requires the estimated delay and correction applied of
blockette 57 to be positive and nearly equal.  evalresp needs these
conventions to evaluate a response correctly.  A response that does not follow
them gives wrong results, which may modify data in subtle ways when applied to
a time series.
.SS "FIR filters"
A FIR filter whose delay correction is null is evaluated with no phase shift,
even if it is asymmetric.  Several FIR filters in one stage are joined
into one in the order read: they are the continuation blockettes SEED uses when
the coefficients, more than 415 of them, do not fit in one blockette.  The
filter in the second blockette is assumed to have the same input sample interval
as the first.  FIR filters that are not normalised to 1 at frequency zero are
normalised.
.LP
The delay of an asymmetric FIR filter is the correction applied minus the
calculated delay, or the estimated delay if \fB\-use\-estimated\-delay\fR is given.
The delay is zero for all other filters.
.SS "IIR filters"
IIR filters given as coefficients, which may have a phase shift, are supported.
The coefficients of one stage must be in one blockette.
.SS "LIST BLOCKETTE"
A List blockette (55) gives amplitude and phase at a set of frequencies and is
supported only as stage 1, optionally with a stage 0 gain, and not mixed with
other stages.  By default the frequencies requested are ignored and output is
given at the frequencies of the blockette.
.LP
With \fB\-il\fR or \fB\-ii\fR the blockette is instead interpolated to the
frequencies requested.  The interpolation is a cubic spline in log10 of the
frequency and of the amplitude, so that it cannot overshoot or go negative on
a steep roll\-off; it falls back to linear interpolation, with a warning, if the
list has a frequency or amplitude that is not positive.  Requested frequencies
outside the range of the blockette are dropped, with a warning.  This changes
the output only for responses with a List blockette.
.SS "POLYNOMIAL BLOCKETTE"
A polynomial blockette (62) cannot generally be a Fourier response because it
is non\-linear.  There are two exceptions.  If the polynomial is linear
(y = a x + b), the offset is ignored and the slope b is a gain independent of
frequency, with a phase of 0 degrees if b is positive and 180 degrees if it is
negative.  Otherwise, for a signal that uses only a small part of the curve, the
tangent at the signal value is used as the linear case.
.LP
For f(x) = a0 + a1 x + a2 x^2 + ... + an x^n the slope at X is the sum, for i = 1
to n, of i * ai * X^(i\-1).  X is given with \fB\-b62_x\fR, in counts if the
blockette is the only one in the response and in volts if it is part of a cascade.
The amplitude of the result depends on it.  X defaults to 0, where the slope is
a1.  \fB\-b62_x\fR is not needed for
responses without a polynomial blockette.
.SS "Unexpected cases"
.IP \(bu 2
Stand\-alone FIR filters, with no sample rate or gain, are discarded.  Only
that stage is dropped; the rest of the response is evaluated.
.IP \(bu
An IIR coefficients filter in a stage with more than one blockette 54 is not
supported.
.IP \(bu
A List stage mixed with other stages in one response is not supported.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
To evaluate channel BH1 of station ANMO on day 100 of 2016, at 50 frequencies
spaced logarithmically from 0.01 to 10 Hz:
.IP
evalresp ANMO BH1 2016 100 0.01 10 50 \-f station.xml
.LP
If the file holds the channel at locations 00 and 10, this writes
AMP.IU.ANMO.00.BH1, PHASE.IU.ANMO.00.BH1, AMP.IU.ANMO.10.BH1 and
PHASE.IU.ANMO.10.BH1.  To evaluate only location 00, in complex spectra:
.IP
evalresp ANMO BH1 2016 100 0.01 10 50 \-f station.xml \-l 00 \-r cs
.LP
To evaluate all the broadband and long\-period channels of two stations from a
directory of legacy RESP files, with a summary of each response:
.IP
evalresp HRV,ANMO 'BH?,LH?' 1992 231 0.001 10 100 \-f /home/RESP/NEW \-t 12:31:04 \-v
.LP
The quotes stop the shell from expanding the question marks.  This writes an
AMP and a PHASE file for each of the channels found, such as
AMP.IU.HRV..BHE and PHASE.IU.HRV..BHE.
.LP
To write amplitude and phase to a pipe:
.IP
evalresp ANMO BHZ 2010 1 0.001 10 100 \-stdio < RESP.IU.ANMO.00.BHZ
.LP
The files can be read by any graphing program that reads columns of numbers.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fIxml2resp\fR(1), \fIrdseed\fR(1)
